<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Niumi</h1><p class="st-meta">1450 CE – 1897 CE (approx.) · capital Essau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mandinka kingdom at the mouth of the Gambia river, whose rulers taxed European ships and traders for four centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Niumi, called Barra by Europeans, was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> kingdoms founded along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> river as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> influence spread westward, probably by the 15th century. It occupied the north bank at the river mouth, including the villages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juffure">Juffure</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albreda">Albreda</a>, opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunta-kinteh-island">James Island</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a> was chosen in rotation from royal lineages based in several towns, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essau">Essau</a> as the royal seat. Niumi collected customs from European ships entering the river and supplied food, water and slaves. English and French posts on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunta-kinteh-island">James Island</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albreda">Albreda</a> depended on its goodwill.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1826 Niumi ceded a strip of land, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceded-mile">Ceded Mile</a>, to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, and in 1831 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barra-war">Barra War</a> between Niumi and the British ended in its defeat. It was made part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gambia-colony-and-protectorate">Gambia Colony and Protectorate</a> in the 1890s and is now a district of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-niumi.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Niumi" width="320" height="209"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Kingdom_of_Niumi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1450 CE – 1897 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Essau</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam and Mandinka traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mandinka</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/gambia">Gambia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Niumi, Barra, Nomi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1674&amp;focus=kingdom-of-niumi" aria-label="Kingdom of Niumi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4227 1675 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Niumi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4547 1931L4769 1989M4547 1931L4546 1933"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4769 1989h0M4546 1933h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4547 1931h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4547 1931h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Essau. <a href="/atlas?year=1674&amp;focus=kingdom-of-niumi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
